# {{ project_name }} — product as code

Your product is defined as a typed, validated graph under [`{{ product_dir }}/`]({{ product_dir }}/),
and generated into [`{{ output_bundle }}`]({{ output_bundle }}) by
[FastPDLC](https://github.com/tarvitave/fastpdlc).

## Try the thing that makes this different

```bash
pip install fastpdlc=={{ fastpdlc_version }}
fastpdlc -p product_hooks.py validate      # passes
```

Now rename `test_the_example_holds_for_empty_input` in
[`tests/test_example.py`](tests/test_example.py) and run it again:

```
ERROR PAC-902 {{ product_dir }}/features/FEAT-example.md: 'test_the_example_holds_for_empty_input'
      is not declared in tests/test_example.py
```

**That is the whole idea.** `FEAT-example` claims a criterion is proven by that test.
The moment the claim stops being true, the build says so — before review, not during
an audit. Rename it back, and it passes again.

Everything else here is in service of that: a schema so a feature cannot be
half-written, cross-references so it cannot point at a term that does not exist, and
a committed bundle so what your docs render is what your repo actually says.

## Workflow

```bash
fastpdlc -p product_hooks.py validate    # the CI gate
fastpdlc -p product_hooks.py build       # regenerate {{ output_bundle }} after an edit
fastpdlc -p product_hooks.py validate --watch   # re-run it on every save
```

Commit `{{ output_bundle }}` alongside your edits; the `product-validate` workflow
fails any PR where it has drifted out of sync with `{{ product_dir }}/`.

## Adding artifacts

Each file under `{{ product_dir }}/<type>/` is one record. Its `id` must start with
the type's prefix and match the filename (e.g. `{{ product_dir }}/terms/TERM-payment.md`
has `id: TERM-payment`). Reference fields must resolve to a real id.

Two types ship here — a glossary and a feature — because the point lands faster with
two than with eight. [`product.config.yaml`](product.config.yaml) is where you add
more: decisions, constraints, business rules, metrics. Each is about eight lines.

## Your own rules

[`product_hooks.py`](product_hooks.py) holds the checks a config file cannot express —
here, that every acceptance criterion resolves to a test that exists, and that a
`shipped` feature has no unproven criterion. Add your own beside them; the `9xx`
diagnostic range is yours, and `0xx`–`06x` belong to the engine.
